mystar = function(S1, S2){
xbar1 = mean(S1)
xbar2 = mean(S2)
val = 1/(abs(xbar1-xbar2) +2) # Ideal value for not rejection = 1/2 = 0.5
}
set.seed(1234)
S1 = rnorm(17, 21.5, 1.3)
S2 = rnorm(27, 24.3, 1.9)
starstat = mystar(S1, S2)
starstat # 0.2264458
# Assume Null distribution for both is that they are normally distributed
nmc = 100000 # "Normal Monte-Carlo
set.seed(90909)
mcstars = c()
for (k in 1:nmc){
smc1 = rnorm(17, 0, 1) # Use the same n values, n could impact the distribution
smc2 = rnorm(27, 0, 1) # Since we don't know population parameters, use 0 and 1
# IMPORTANT: Standardization can change the ideal value (since mean = 0)
mcval = mystar(smc1, smc2)
mcstars = c(mcstars, mcval)
}
hist(mcstars, breaks=50, col='purple3')
# p val = number of extreme values (in this case less than starstat) against all values
meanstars = mean(mcstars <= starstat)
meanstars
starstat2 = 0.37
mean(mcstars<= starstat2) # Gives p-value, check against the alpha
starstat2 = 0.37
p-val = mean(mcstars<= starstat2) # Gives p-value, check against the alpha
p-val
p-val = mean(mcstars<= starstat2) # Gives p-value, check against the alpha
p_val = mean(mcstars<= starstat2) # Gives p-value, check against the alpha
p_val
Fx = seq(from = 1, to=5, by=1)/ (5+1) # Fx = Empirical Cumulative Density Function (CDF)
zx_true = qnorm(Fx, 0, 1, lower.tail=T)
Fx
